    "evidence_contract": "solve_question:1",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-333345",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/51ffadb1da41e6a1fe06fdc9c14a6967d3a80b4d26c6a830a401105e0f18bbf5.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$",
