    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21635",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/270b7c6ff796937603596f26e70886f41438ce87c043d3a1b4fc221398b8692f.png)\n\n \n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T,$ respectively. \n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1,$ $\\lambda_2,$ and $0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/c8e2c43932588f0c7b6626b4a39f23eb7e2612f0361b2c1b45569dba06f69d8d.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/8d94e61fd6ff7b8de25975567c79aafb6d32db722bd2fc8bf514c3fe848df6c0.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/d241239c6475fbcf9559c2fcbbb8ca2805a6692fd1a9b3966fe8b38441075a4d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/f1919306b613e87d45bb4043debbe74c13725344d20e1b2bb36d75e8882da95c.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 >  0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/322927882b291942fd7d3be783d9c41f006254f6698a6f6ae16e2e6ee3cd0c3a.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 0,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/fb40117d63e76b2b2db59313b173b04a9a23ef2a28493a5041584e4c7c60c67d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217341/assets/88e3c149e67359fd5cdd5a0938f247eac64783567184780fd3a1a873e6f9dcc4.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 > 0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_2.$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda_2 > \\lambda_1 > 0.$",
